ed-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $8$ does not take the above form. The value $8$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{8}{1},$ and the numerator $(8)$ is not a factor of $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-6667/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-6667"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83828/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "8d49b2c8-f558-5c0d-9ae7-dfb73df7ea3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83828/selection/718ff1f1e9146b5d5d2cf798138ec0393245e544620d23b1f48534b92efd546a" :answer/id #uuid "8f673457-949d-5d10-b5cd-f2c7103570e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83828/selection/b5672e3d5d727bcb709f33eeb0f8df47c72e1fe5fb135d6d9ea4ceefea5f0aa1" :answer/id #uuid "708fffb9-5c5a-5d1d-a413-d5d2fe1273d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83828/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "093cb307-2339-5df3-b7ab-709b897040e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83828/selection/471d022dc8477dee5b5835181abb5643fcc83818c373aaad08fa968767db722e" :answer/id #uuid "de880298-884b-5125-ab73-cba4a6788362" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83828/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e009c02-4b65-5e34-b4fc-c0ea72969fb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83828/selection/471d022dc8477dee5b5835181abb5643fcc83818c373aaad08fa968767db722e" :answer-field/choices ["q-83828/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-83828/selection/718ff1f1e9146b5d5d2cf798138ec0393245e544620d23b1f48534b92efd546a" "q-83828/selection/b5672e3d5d727bcb709f33eeb0f8df47c72e1fe5fb135d6d9ea4ceefea5f0aa1" "q-83828/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" "q-83828/selection/471d022dc8477dee5b5835181abb5643fcc83818c373aaad08fa968767db722e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83828" :question/id #uuid "44ccdd89-c6f2-563b-8fc8-98dee7c84a8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83828" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-14x+13?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $13$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }13}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{13}{6}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(6)$ is not a factor of $8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83828/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83828"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-323215/field-1/286fb92e9ba180b012d241bb7e762701483c9c864420d9df6597147f2e2fb458" :answer/id #uuid "124ff803-b147-51a0-97d6-091acd1bb82e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323215/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9beaac9b-a826-51d9-b65f-a90fc0d692f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323215/field-1/286fb92e9ba180b012d241bb7e762701483c9c864420d9df6597147f2e2fb458" :answer-field/choices ["q-323215/field-1/286fb92e9ba180b012d241bb7e762701483c9c864420d9df6597147f2e2fb458"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323215" :question/id #uuid "f1f33d61-0d80-57df-8e34-bef51b594085" :question/math-academy-id "q-323215" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=16{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+7x+12$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 6,$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 8,$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\pm \\frac{1}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\  & \\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 3,\\text{ }\\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 6,\\text{ }\\pm 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $28$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 14=28.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-323215/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-323215"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-323222/field-1/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "b2479bc9-6b2a-5385-99d3-9a1dbb7c48f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323222/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "02df68ea-79bd-51b8-b773-8398857a4514" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323222/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9da605cb-ae41-5ee2-8d88-220805e3f6a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323222/field-1/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-323222/field-1/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-323222/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323222" :question/id #uuid "95007f34-4662-5e62-8850-de706768b936" :question/math-academy-id "q-323222" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-5x-9$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-9,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 3$, and $\\pm 9.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-323222/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-323222"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-323231/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "24dff74e-d0ac-51aa-959d-4864d68db61c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323231/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "903ad97d-d306-5752-bf8f-9d4447c8744e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323231/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-323231/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323231" :question/id #uuid "d7521cae-1b98-544e-8948-197abd4b00f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-323231" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-5x+10$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{10}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 5, & \\pm 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-323231/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-323231"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14144/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "962dcb60-fc4f-5978-af78-68be7f79b1b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14144/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "dcbb019f-3b3a-5880-9058-8fe3b82ec1e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14144/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "310645d4-04fb-5e53-a610-278fff63446f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14144/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" :answer/id #uuid "7c1a8b22-b7c6-5a2f-9bc6-1ee8e1e5e0e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14144/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "b15a90db-be51-5ab6-9ef8-0c1889e621f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14144/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6460732-eee0-50fa-a379-80c2267a6f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-14144/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-14144/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-14144/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-14144/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-14144/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" "q-14144/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14144" :question/id #uuid "3b022a96-d200-55d8-b457-6c4636e4afcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-14144" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{8}-18{x}^{2}-33x-18,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $3,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 3.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{18}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm 3, \\\\ \\pm 18, & \\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-14144/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14144"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112966/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "1ba3b5eb-68c3-5d1a-a925-e179e3828246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112966/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "3dda3f2c-5004-5659-9106-0a21dd38de91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112966/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "e91456c2-9271-5b13-9277-3fd1f75ff31c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112966/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "592168c9-69e7-5c37-b75f-823c75abded4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112966/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "ef9e712a-f023-5a7a-bf1c-5c1c026ec7fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112966/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "97157662-5ee7-5d66-aa2a-b74a821e6a41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112966/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-112966/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" "q-112966/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-112966/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-112966/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-112966/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112966" :question/id #uuid "eb7cad97-7ee5-555b-ba50-25e926edbf58" :question/math-academy-id "q-112966" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{8}-9{x}^{4}-2x+14,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112966/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112966"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112989/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "97c9c793-3414-53db-b6aa-cb0f04d28589" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112989/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "8c9cbc41-20e9-57f3-a95e-a6e00a16dea7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112989/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "61ca4279-fe02-501e-8d9b-e4b2df1b454b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112989/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "4968a692-b96b-545e-846e-99000647d563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112989/selection/35f5572ccca3a8c38d3ca06e2a92b51065f48e45d29833e19f4eb153cae096fc" :answer/id #uuid "5d258c3e-e49f-50fc-9bc7-8eb4770d54d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112989/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "90d0b1e4-33c1-57ab-bca5-cb616e35952f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112989/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-112989/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-112989/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-112989/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-112989/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-112989/selection/35f5572ccca3a8c38d3ca06e2a92b51065f48e45d29833e19f4eb153cae096fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112989" :question/id #uuid "17fefc5a-b0f8-553d-8d51-9bbf7ea2fa88" :question/math-academy-id "q-112989" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+1$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $1$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }1}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{4},1)$ is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $8$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $8$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{1}{2})(8{x}^{2}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{1}{2})(8{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{1}{2})(4{x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =(2x-1)(2x+1)(2x-1) \\\\  & =(2x+1)(2x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-112989/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112989"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39036/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer/id #uuid "9f620bbb-ded2-5b62-865e-cb5759e9742d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39036/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer/id #uuid "b95d111e-f7d7-5ca0-b4b8-5542af347a29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39036/selection/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer/id #uuid "be6c9307-6adc-5a44-b26c-474ba74b7677" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39036/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "fa654955-243e-5212-9d47-7f0c689cb259" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39036/selection/f4f7e304b38327904d9fdebbac3479cccadb547b1c29d7b8e5938e7012f9d65b" :answer/id #uuid "819d3f2d-23e5-5cfb-9fe3-c30be4ff81a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39036/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8180927-f275-598f-a83f-bc3c11ee875c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39036/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer-field/choices ["q-39036/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" "q-39036/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" "q-39036/selection/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" "q-39036/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-39036/selection/f4f7e304b38327904d9fdebbac3479cccadb547b1c29d7b8e5938e7012f9d65b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39036" :question/id #uuid "9a3e4429-09a5-5d58-a296-a07129c659bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39036" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+35{x}^{2}+2x+14$ has a root in the interval $(-15,-6),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $14$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }14}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 7,\\pm 14,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\pm \\frac{7}{5},\\pm \\frac{14}{5}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-15,-6),$ are $-14$ and $-7.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+7)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-7$ | $5$ | $35$ | $2$ | $14$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-35$ | $0$ | $-14$ |\n|  | $5$ | $0$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+7)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+7)(5{x}^{2}+2).\n$$\n\nSince $5{x}^{2}+2\\ne 0$ for any real number $x,$ the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=-7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-39036/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39036"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-39033"] :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-15x-25$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $3$ | $5$ | $-15$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $25$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-38251/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "f6e36e09-a73b-5c2e-8d50-46c4ff381cda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38251/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "d86ad3cb-810a-5b83-8ebc-83b2d5440f33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38251/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "ba2446a4-ddce-59f6-a0e1-7a75a35b0f76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38251/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "2904b527-24b0-53da-a07b-185b187753de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38251/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "17db9749-e188-5b23-92f3-a51beda3f1be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38251/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "17a6ea11-34b9-55b9-b0bf-f1e42d63bdc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-38251/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-38251/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-38251/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-38251/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-38251/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-38251/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-38251" :question/id #uuid "2adddd45-7f27-54e7-9dbc-a6a10272c38e" :question/math-academy-id "q-38251" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}+31x-15$ has one of its roots in the interval $(1,2),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-15$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-15}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 3,\\pm 5,\\pm 15,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\pm \\frac{15}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\pm \\frac{5}{4},\\pm \\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(1,2),$ are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $4$ | $-20$ | $31$ | $-15$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $6$ | $-21$ | $15$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-14$ | $10$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{3}{2})(4{x}^{2}-14x+10).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(4{x}^{2}-14x+10) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-7x+5) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2x-5)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},1.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}+1=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-38251/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38251"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-323610/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "98b60f68-8758-5a21-9852-74557299ddcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323610/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2039748-636f-5a6a-9ec7-df6cdf6723cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323610/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-323610/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323610/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "e02738fe-87ad-5069-8aa4-3e0eced43d48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323610/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb121a5-e119-5d0c-933f-f2fc00e26b1a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323610/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-323610/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323610/field-3/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "967b2bb0-0e33-5e7b-926a-0b782b7d34b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323610/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "e2198240-2089-5c73-9517-53512a0cf04c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323610/field-3/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-323610/field-3/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323610" :question/id #uuid "7638975a-f83e-5fc9-ae9e-a40228be8f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-323610" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-10x-8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(3,5).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(3,5)$ is $4.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-10$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-323610/field-1" "q-323610/field-2" "q-323610/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-323610"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7378"] :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 9x^4-3x^2-7x-6?$\n\n- $-\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\n- $-9$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-6,$ and the leading coefficient is $9.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{-6}}{\\textrm{factor of }9}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(2)$ is a factor of $-6$ and the denominator $(3)$ is a factor of $9.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7365"] :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 12x^4+5x^3-6x+15?$\n\n- $\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n- $-\\dfrac{3}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $15$ and the leading coefficient is $12.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{15}}{\\textrm{factor of }12}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\dfrac 23$ does not take the above form. The numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $15.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5767"] :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6x^4-7x^3+11x-9,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-9,$ and its factors are $\\pm1,$ $\\pm3,$ and $\\pm9.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\; \\pm \\frac13, \\; \\pm \\frac12, \\; \\pm 1, \\; \\pm \\frac32, \\; \\pm 3, \\; \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, \\; \\pm 9.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm$ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2 \\cdot 8 = 16.$)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7413"] :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x) =x^3 - x^2 - 4x + 4$ has a root in the interval $(0,4),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{4}}{\\textrm{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,4),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\left( x - 1\\right) (x^2 - 4).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2, 1, 2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+1+2 =1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
